==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₃reǵ-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|rectifien}}, from {{der|en|xno|rectifiier}}, {{m|fro|rectefier|t=to make straight}}, from {{der|en|ML.|rēctificō|t=to make right}}, from {{der|en|la|rēctus|t=straight}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈɹɛktəˌfaɪ/}}
* {{audio|en|en-GB-rectify.ogg|Audio (UK)}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|obsolete|transitive}} To [[heal]] (an organ or part of the body). {{defdate|14th|18th c.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[restore]] (someone or something) to its proper condition; to straighten out, to set right. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[remedy]] or [[fix]] (an undesirable state of affairs, situation etc.). {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|to '''rectify''' the crisis}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|chemistry}} To [[purify]] or [[refine]] (a substance) by [[distillation]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[correct]] or [[amend]] (a mistake, defect etc.). {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|now|rare}} To [[correct]] (someone who is mistaken). {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
#* '''1646''', {{w|Sir Thomas Browne}}, ''Pseudodoxia Epidemica'', I.3:
#*: For thus their Sense informeth them, and herein their Reason cannot '''Rectifie''' them; and therefore hopelessly continuing in mistakes, they live and die in their absurdities {{...}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|geodesy|historical}} To adjust (a globe or sundial) to prepare for the solution of a proposed problem. {{defdate|from 16th c.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|electronics}} To [[convert]] ([[alternating current]]) into [[direct current]]. {{defdate|from 19th c.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|math}} To determine the length of a [[curve]] included between two [[limit]]s.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To produce (as factitious gin or brandy) by redistilling bad wines or strong spirits (whisky, rum, etc.) with flavourings.
{{attention|en|There is at least one missing sense from geometry: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rectification_(geometry)}}

